\cf0 Night three was when everything fell apart.\
\
Not because of the altitude.\
\
Not because of the cold.\
\
Not because William, despite being on a post-grad mountain-climbing trip, was technically not actually post-grad.\
\
The disaster was simpler.\
\
They ran out of beer.\
\
Gabe discovered it first.\
\
He unzipped the backpack, stared into the empty cooler compartment, and went completely still.\
\
\'93No.\'94\
\
William looked up from where he was trying to convince Colin that his unfinished degree was \'93more of a philosophical issue than an academic one.\'94\
\
\'93What?\'94\
\
\'93We have no beer.\'94\
\
Silence fell over the campsite.\
\
The wind whipped through the mountains.\
\
Somewhere in the darkness, a bird made a noise that sounded vaguely disappointed.\
\
William frowned.\
\
\'93That\'92s impossible.\'94\
\
Gabe wordlessly held up the final empty can.\
\
William stared.\
\
Colin stared.\
\
The realization settled over all three of them like a funeral shroud.\
\
\'93Oh,\'94 William said quietly.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
The fight began twenty seconds later.\
\
\'93This is your fault.\'94\
\
\'93My fault?\'94\
\
\'93You argued for one suitcase.\'94\
\
\'93Because carrying two suitcases up a mountain is insane.\'94\
\
\'93It would\'92ve been worth it.\'94\
\
\'93For beer?\'94\
\
\'93For enough beer.\'94\
\
William stood.\
\
\'93You wanted to bring forty-eight cans.\'94\
\
\'93We DID bring forty-eight cans.\'94\
\
\'93And somehow you\'92re acting like we planned poorly.\'94\
\
\'93Because we did.\'94\
\
The argument intensified.\
\
Soon they were yelling over each other with the energy of a long-married couple disputing a dishwasher.\
\
Colin sat on a rock eating trail mix and watching the exchange unfold.\
\
Occasionally he attempted mediation.\
\
Neither acknowledged his existence.\
\
Eventually he gave up.\
\
\'93Neptune would\'92ve appreciated me,\'94 he muttered.\
\
Colin was beginning to suspect he\'92d been invited solely to prevent the appearance of a honeymoon.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
An hour later William and Gabe sat on opposite sides of the campsite.\
\
Neither was speaking.\
\
Neither was looking at the other.\
\
Both were aggressively pretending not to care.\
\
The atmosphere was so tense it could\'92ve been bottled and sold as a military-grade adhesive.\
\
Colin occupied the unfortunate space between them.\
\
To his left sat Gabe, dramatically staring into the darkness.\
\
To his right sat William, equally dramatically staring into slightly different darkness.\
\
Colin sighed.\
\
\'93I feel like I\'92m the child in a divorce.\'94\
\
No response.\
\
He sighed again.\
\
\'93I miss Neptune.\'94\
\
Still nothing.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
The silence lasted another forty minutes.\
\
Then William finally cracked.\
\
It happened because Gabe coughed.\
\
Not a concerning cough.\
\
Not even a particularly noticeable cough.\
\
Just a cough.\
\
William immediately looked over.\
\
\'93You okay?\'94\
\
Gabe turned toward him.\
\
For several seconds neither spoke.\
\
Then Gabe crossed his arms.\
\
\'93You started it.\'94\
\
William rolled his eyes.\
\
\'93You called me irresponsible.\'94\
\
\'93You ARE irresponsible.\'94\
\
\'93You\'92re the one who wanted to haul sixty pounds of beer up a volcano.\'94\
\
\'93It would\'92ve solved the current situation.\'94\
\
William opened his mouth.\
\
Paused.\
\
Then laughed.\
\
Just a little.\
\
Against his will.\
\
Gabe tried not to laugh too.\
\
Failed instantly.\
\
Within thirty seconds they were both grinning.\
\
The fight collapsed under its own absurdity.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
\'93I\'92m sorry,\'94 William finally said.\
\
Gabe nodded.\
\
\'93I\'92m sorry too.\'94\
\
A pause.\
\
Then Gabe pointed accusingly.\
\
\'93You are still a trigger. It\'92s not my fault we fought. You\'92re just more invested than I am. And I miss\'94\
\
William accepted this with the weary resignation of a man hearing a familiar weather report.\
\
\'93Fair.\'94\
\
Another pause.\
\
The stars stretched endlessly above them.\
\
The mountain sat silent around them.\
\
Colin watched the interaction unfold with the expression of someone observing a rare wildlife mating ritual.\
\
Finally Gabe sighed.\
\
\'93Love you, bro.\'94\
\
William answered immediately.\
\
Not a second of hesitation.\
\
Not a moment of consideration.\
\
The response came with the speed of muscle memory.\
\
\'93Love you too, bro.\'94\
\
Colin slowly lowered the granola bar he had been eating.\
\
\'93Jesus Christ.\'94\
\
\'93Don\'92t say the Lord\'92s name in vain. I can\'92t go to church on this mountain.\'94 William said, but his smile was bright like a bag of diamonds.\
\
\uc0\u11835 \
\
Later that night the temperature dropped sharply.\
\
The three of them crawled into their tents.\
\
From somewhere in the darkness Colin heard Gabe and William continuing a conversation that had apparently resumed exactly where it left off before the fight.\
\
Their voices drifted across the campsite.\
\
Occasional laughter.\
\
Occasional arguments.\
\
Occasional declarations of brotherly affection.\
\
Colin stared at the ceiling of his tent.\
\
Then at the empty space beside him.\
\
Then at the stars visible through the mesh overhead.\
\
\'93You know,\'94 he said to nobody in particular, \'93Neptune would\'92ve slept on the floor with me.\'94\
\
The mountain offered no response.\
\
From the neighboring tent came the sound of Gabe laughing at something William had said.\
\
Colin closed his eyes.\
\
Night three was going great.}